# Citation metadata. GitHub reads this file to offer "Cite this repository",
# and Zenodo reads it when archiving a release.
#
# At each release, update `version` and `date-released`, and add the new
# version DOI under `identifiers`. The `doi` below is the concept DOI: it
# always resolves to the latest version, and is the one to cite unless a
# particular release is meant.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "energese: Odum's Energy Systems Language as a LuaLaTeX package"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Sholto
    family-names: Maud
    email: sholto.maud@gmail.com
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "https://github.com/energese-project/latex-energese"
license: MIT
version: v0.1.0
date-released: "2026-09-06"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22340275
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22340275
    description: "Concept DOI, resolving to the latest version"
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22340276
    description: "DOI for version v0.0.1"
  # Each release mints its own version DOI once Zenodo has archived it; add
  # the new one here after publishing, alongside the concept DOI above.
abstract: >-
  energese renders H. T. Odum's Energy Systems Language (ESL) diagrams from a
  declarative model, in JSON or in TeX, as a LuaLaTeX package. Symbol geometry
  is measured from Odum's published symbol sheet and checked against it on every
  build; layout is derived from the model, placing components by transformity
  and routing pathways around the symbols they do not connect to. A diagram is
  therefore a function of its model rather than a drawing maintained beside it,
  which is what lets a published figure be regenerated when the model changes.
keywords:
  - Energy Systems Language
  - systems ecology
  - H.T. Odum
  - emergy
  - transformity
  - LaTeX
  - LuaLaTeX
  - TikZ
  - scientific figures
  - reproducible research
  - diagram generation
